[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Federation & Microservices Architecture in GraphQL"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"As organizations scale, monolithic GraphQL schemas can become difficult to maintain across multiple autonomous teams. GraphQL Federation solves this architectural bottleneck by allowing decoupled microservices (subgraphs) to define their own GraphQL schemas, which an API Gateway composes into a single, unified supergraph for clients."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Core Concepts of GraphQL Federation"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Supergraph Gateway: The single entry point that receives client GraphQL queries, creates execution plans, and orchestrates requests across underlying microservices."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Subgraphs: Independent GraphQL microservices that own specific domain logic and entities."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Entities: GraphQL object types that can be extended across multiple subgraphs using primary keys."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Key Federation Directives"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"Federation uses declarative schema directives to establish relationships between microservices without creating direct tight coupling:"},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"@key(fields: 'id'): Specifies the primary entity key used by other subgraphs to reference and fetch this type."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"@external: Indicates that a field is defined and owned by another subgraph."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"@requires(fields: '...'): Declares that a resolver depends on specific external fields from another service."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"@provides(fields: '...'): Optimizes nested queries by asserting that a subgraph can provide an external field."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Implementing Subgraphs"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"In a federated environment, a base subgraph defines an entity, while other subgraphs extend it. Below is an example of a Users service exposing a User entity."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"type User @key(fields: "id") {
  id: ID!
  username: String!
  email: String!
}

type Query {
  user(id: ID!): User
}"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"Next, a separate Reviews subgraph extends the User entity to add a list of reviews associated with that user."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"type Review {
  id: ID!
  content: String!
  rating: Int!
  author: User!
}

extend type User @key(fields: "id") {
  id: ID! @external
  reviews: [Review]
}"},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"Configuring the API Gateway"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Gateway orchestrates queries across subgraphs transparently. Here is how to configure an Apollo Gateway instance using Node.js:"},{"order":20,"type":"code","text":"const { ApolloServer } = require('@apollo/server');
const { startStandaloneServer } = require('@apollo/server/standalone');
const { ApolloGateway, IntrospectAndCompose } = require('@apollo/gateway');

const gateway = new ApolloGateway({
  supergraphSdl: new IntrospectAndCompose({
    subgraphs: [
      { name: 'users', url: 'http://localhost:4001/graphql' },
      { name: 'reviews', url: 'http://localhost:4002/graphql' },
    ],
  }),
});

const server = new ApolloServer({ gateway });

startStandaloneServer(server, { listen: { port: 4000 } }).then(({ url }) => {
  console.log('Gateway running at ' + url);
});"},{"order":21,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for Federated Architectures"},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Domain-Driven Subgraphs: Design subgraph boundaries based on business domains rather than database structures."},{"order":23,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Schema Registries & CI/CD: Use a schema registry (such as Apollo GraphOS or Hive) to run composition checks in CI/CD before deploying breaking schema changes."},{"order":24,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Fault Tolerance: Implement timeouts and fallback policies in the Gateway so that a single failing subgraph does not break the entire GraphQL execution."}]
